-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Buffer Overflow:** The code checks `count >= maxCount - 3` before drawing to ensure space for a triangle. If exceeded, it flushes immediately [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Ambiguity:** Cases 46, 47, 98, 99, 210, 213, 214, 215 contain saddle points. The standard LUT uses a fixed triangulation, which can cause cracks if adjacent voxels resolve ambiguity differently [10327-lookup-table-and-ambiguity-handling].
-*   **Precision:** Floating-point interpolation errors may cause small gaps at high resolutions.
+### Parameters and Edge Cases
+*   **maxCount**: Set to 4096 in the reference implementation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Buffer Overflow**: The check `count >= maxCount - 3` ensures there is space for a full triangle (3 vertices) before adding it. If this condition is met, the buffer is flushed immediately [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Ambiguity**: Cases 46, 47, 98, 99, 210, 213, 214, 215 may produce topological errors if not handled. The standard LUT is used, which may cause cracks if adjacent voxels resolve ambiguities inconsistently [Lookup Table and Ambiguity Handling](10327-lookup-table-and-ambiguity-handling.md) [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Limited by CPU bottlenecks at high resolutions or high blob counts [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, easy to debug, serves as a reference for GPU implementations.
-*   **Limitations:** Poor scalability due to CPU-bound field evaluation and geometry generation. High CPU-GPU transfer overhead if not batched effectively [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Reference implementation, low-resolution previews, or educational purposes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** High-resolution real-time rendering, large datasets [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N_x N_y N_z)$ for field generation and mesh extraction. The constant factor is high due to CPU-bound scalar field computation and per-vertex overhead.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N_x N_y N_z)$ for the scalar field grid. The immediate mode buffer is fixed at `maxCount` vertices, independent of scene size, but limits the number of triangles rendered per frame to `maxCount / 3`.
+*   **Performance**: Limited by CPU bottlenecks at high resolutions or high blob counts. It is significantly slower than GPU-based methods for large grids [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Simple implementation, serves as a good reference, minimal GPU memory usage for mesh storage.
+*   **Limitations**: Poor scalability, high CPU usage, limited by `maxCount` per frame (requires multiple draw calls for large scenes).
 
 ## Background
-The CPU Immediate Mode Marching Cubes implementation is inspired by Gregg Tavares' (Greggman) metaballs demo [S1, unofficial]. It uses classic Marching Cubes tables from Paul Bourke [S1, unofficial]. The project is part of the `WebGL2-GPU-Marching-Cubes` repository by 3merillon [S1, unofficial].
